## 4. Transcripts (Console Output) Included

All transcripts are in the `demo/` directory of the repository. They were
recorded with `script -c "make qemu" demo/DemoN.txt`, which captures both the
commands typed and the resulting kernel output.

| File | Scenario | What it shows |
|---|---|---|
| `demo/Demo1.txt` | 1 — boot with mask 0 | Kernel boots to a shell; `ls` and `echo hi` run with zero debug output. `dbg` reports `mask 0x0 (none)`. |
| `demo/Demo2.txt` | 2 — `dbg on proc`, then `ls` and `echo hi` | 15 lifecycle records covering the full fork/exec/exit/wait/free chain of each command, with parent and child pids agreeing. |
| `demo/Demo3.txt` | 3 — `forktest` | 62 processes created and torn down. No leaks, no double-frees. |
| `demo/Demo4.txt` | 4 — instrumented workload, then `dbg off all`, then the same workload | 18 lifecycle records before the mask is cleared, complete silence after. |
| `demo/Demo5.txt` | 5 — `dbg only sched`, `dbg level trace` | The context-switch flood. See §5.5 for how this message would be made usable. |
| `demo/Demo6.txt` | 6 — `usertests` at mask 0 | The full test suite with the facility compiled in but disabled. |

### Scenario 1 — silent boot

With `debug_mask` at its default of 0, the kernel boots to the shell and runs a
normal workload without emitting a single debug line. Nothing needs to be turned
off first; the facility is inert until a category is enabled.

### Scenario 2 — parent and child pids line up

```
$ ls
[proc   ][ 2][allocproc] allocated pid 4
[proc   ][ 2][kfork] child pid 4 created, sz 20480
[proc   ][ 4][kexec] exec ls, 1 args, sz 20480
[proc   ][ 4][kexit] exiting, status 0, 0 children reparented to init
[proc   ][ 2][kwait] reaped pid 4, status 0
[proc   ][ 2][freeproc] freeing slot of pid 4 (ls)
```

The bracketed number in the prefix is the pid of the *running* process. The
shell is pid 2: it allocates the slot, forks, and later reaps and frees the
child. The child, pid 4, is the process that execs and exits. The two views
agree on the pid throughout, which is what makes the chain reconstructable.

### Scenario 3 — no leaks, no double-frees

Counting the lifecycle records in `demo/Demo3.txt`:

| Measure | Count |
|---|---|
| `allocated pid` records | 62 (62 distinct pids) |
| `freeing slot of pid` records | 63 (63 distinct pids) |
| Allocated but never freed | none |
| Freed more than once | none |
| Exit events / reap events | 63 / 63 |
| `no free proc slot` warnings | 1 |

Every pid that was allocated was subsequently freed exactly once, and no pid was
freed twice.

The apparent asymmetry — 63 frees against 62 allocations — is pid 3, which is
freed but never appears as allocated. Pid 3 *is* the `dbg on proc` process
itself: the shell allocated it before the mask was set, so its allocation
predates logging being enabled while its teardown is recorded. This is the
expected consequence of enabling instrumentation at runtime, not a missing
record.

The single `no free proc slot` warning is `forktest` deliberately exhausting the
process table, which is what that test exists to exercise. The warning fires at
`DBG_WARN`, which is the correct level for resource exhaustion that is not a
bug.

### Scenario 4 — silence

The transcript runs `ls` and `echo hi` with `proc` enabled, issues
`dbg off all`, then runs the identical workload again. After the mask is
cleared, the second pass produces only the commands' own output:

```
$ dbg off all
mask  0x0 (none)
level 3 (info)
$ ls
...directory listing only...
$ echo hi
hi
```

The `dbg off all` command's own fork and exec are still logged, because the mask
is not cleared until that process reaches the system call. Everything after that
point is silent.

### Scenario 5 — the scheduler message

```
$ echo hi
[sched  ][ -][scheduler] switching to pid 2 (sh)
[sched  ][ -][scheduler] switching to pid 5 (sh)
[sched  ][ -][scheduler] switching to pid 5 (sh)
[sched  ][ -][scheduler] switching to pid 5 (sh)
[sched  ][ -][scheduler] switching to pid 5 (sh)
[sched  ][ -][scheduler] switching to pid 5 (sh)
[sched  ][ -][scheduler] switching to pid 5 (sh)
hi
[sched  ][ -][scheduler] switching to pid 2 (sh)
```

A single `echo hi` produces eleven context-switch records, most of them
repeating the same pid. The pid in the prefix is `-` because `myproc()` is null
inside the scheduler; the pid being switched *to* is in the message body
instead. §5.5 explains how this would be made usable.

### Scenario 6 — `usertests` at mask 0

`usertests` was run with the facility compiled in and the mask at 0. No `[proc`
or `[sched` records appear anywhere in the transcript, confirming that the
instrumentation imposes no behavioral change when disabled.

The transcript contains many lines of the form:

```
test kernmem: usertrap(): unexpected scause 0xd pid=6474
            sepc=0x1b54 stval=0x80000000
```

These are **expected**, not failures. Several `usertests` cases — `kernmem`,
`MAXVAplus`, `nowrite`, `stacktest`, `sbrkbugs`, `lazy_unmap` — deliberately
trigger faults to verify that the kernel rejects invalid accesses. Each such
block ends in `OK`. The messages come from stock xv6's `usertrap()` and are
unrelated to this assignment's instrumentation.

